DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The relationship between historical materialism and theology in Benjamin's thought is organized
around the famous puppet-and-dwarf image of Thesis I: historical materialism can win every
game if it enlists theology as its hidden helper. Scholars divide sharply over what this image
implies. One camp (represented by Lowy, Tiedemann, Habermas) reads Benjamin as primarily a
heterodox Marxist who deploys theological concepts instrumentally to revitalize a flagging
materialist critique of progress and Social Democracy's reformism. A second camp (Scholem,
Jacobson, Bielik-Robson) finds the theological commitments -- Jewish messianism, the
Theological-Political Fragment -- to be primary, with Marxism serving as the outer political
idiom for a fundamentally religious vision. A third, more recent position (Khatib, Marramao,
List) attempts to hold both sides in a productive tension, arguing that Benjamin's innovation
lies precisely in the indissolubility of the two registers: "the messianic without messianism"
or a materialist theology that is neither orthodox Marxism nor orthodox Judaism.

A key focal text alongside the Theses is the "Theological-Political Fragment" (c. 1920-21),
whose argument that the profane order and the messianic kingdom run in opposed directions yet
paradoxically amplify each other continues to generate interpretive controversy. Tagliacozzo
reads it through Kant; Moses reads it through Rosenzweig; Fenves through the shape of time.
Benjamin's critique of Social Democracy and orthodox (Second International) Marxism in the
Theses -- specifically their shared faith in progress, evolutionary development, and a linear
concept of historical time -- is understood by most scholars (Lowy, Traverso) as the
political-polemical heart of the work, directed as much against the SPD's accommodation with
Nazism via the Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact period as against theoretical Marxist teleology.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
These debates are central to any comprehensive account of Benjamin's philosophy of history:
the puppet-dwarf image of Thesis I sets the terms for the entire work, and whether theology
is "master," "servant," or irreducibly entangled with materialism determines how one reads
the Theses' concepts of Jetztzeit, messianic arrest, weak messianic power, and the tradition
of the oppressed. The project's coverage of Benjamin's philosophy of history requires taking
a position on this divide.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The reception of Benjamin's critique of Social Democracy specifically in relation to the SPD's
politics in 1939-40 is underexplored in the English-language literature. The relation between
the Theological-Political Fragment's "happiness" argument and the Theses' "weak messianic
power" also remains contested and deserves more systematic treatment.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The key debate to structure synthesis around is the question of which pole -- materialism or
theology -- is primary (or whether the question itself is misframed). Lowy and List provide
useful entry points for the Marxist reading; Jacobson and Marramao for the theological and
post-religious readings respectively; Khatib for the view that the question itself must be
deconstructed.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Theology as hidden engine / Jewish messianism primary: 3 papers (Jacobson, Marramao, Goldstein)
- Heterodox Marxism with theological instruments: 4 papers (Lowy Fire Alarm, Lowy Jewish Radical Culture, List, Khanlarzadeh)
- Deconstruction of the materialism/theology binary: 3 papers (Khatib messianic without messianism, Khatib Benjamin's materialism, Tagliacozzo)
- Reception and comparative studies: 4 papers (Hay, Goldstein, Simpson/Gordon review, Fenves)
====================================================================
}

@book{lowy2005fire,
  author = {L{\"o}wy, Michael},
  title = {Fire Alarm: Reading Walter Benjamin's `On the Concept of History'},
  year = {2005},
  publisher = {Verso},
  address = {London},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: L{\"o}wy provides a thesis-by-thesis commentary on Benjamin's "On the Concept of History," arguing that the Theses represent a unique synthesis of Jewish messianism and Marxist class struggle that constitutes the most radical critique of the ideology of progress in the Western left's tradition. He reads the puppet-and-dwarf image as establishing theology not as mysticism but as a critical energizer that gives historical materialism access to the redemptive charge of past suffering.

  RELEVANCE: This is the most detailed English-language commentary on the Theses and directly addresses every key question in this domain: the relation of materialism to theology, Benjamin's critique of Social Democracy and the SPD, the meaning of weak messianic power, and the political stakes of the work in 1940. It frames the Theses as simultaneously a critique of fascism, Stalinism, and Social Democratic reformism, giving the theological-materialist alliance a specific historical location.

  POSITION: L{\"o}wy reads Benjamin as a heterodox Marxist whose messianism is politically functional rather than theologically dogmatic -- a "romantic anti-capitalist" who found in Jewish eschatology the resources to resist progressivist Marxist orthodoxy.
  },
  keywords = {historical-materialism, theology, Theses-on-history, messianism, Social-Democracy, class-struggle, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{list2020political,
  author = {List, Lotte},
  title = {Political Theology and Historical Materialism: Reading Benjamin against Agamben},
  journal = {Theory, Culture \& Society},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {38},
  number = {3},
  pages = {117--140},
  doi = {10.1177/0263276420936355},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: List argues that Agamben's political defeatism stems from a double misreading: a formalistic reduction of Schmitt and a "Schmittian reduction of Benjamin" that replaces Benjamin's historical materialist critique with messianic nihilism. By recovering Benjamin's own materialism against Agamben's assimilation of it to Schmittian decisionism, she demonstrates that a genuinely radical political theology must remain anchored in historical materialist critique rather than dissolving into juridical abstraction.

  RELEVANCE: This article directly addresses the domain's central question -- how political theology and historical materialism stand to one another in Benjamin -- by arguing that the two cannot be separated but that materialism provides the critical content that prevents theology from becoming apolitical or defeatist. It offers a sharp contrast to Agamben's influential theological reading and reclaims Benjamin as a political thinker.

  POSITION: Benjamin's historical materialism is primary; theology is the motivating force but not the critical substance. The article defends a reading of Benjamin as genuinely Marxist against both theological and post-political receptions.
  },
  keywords = {political-theology, historical-materialism, Agamben, Schmitt, class-struggle, High},
  abstract = {Giorgio Agamben’s work on the power of sovereignty has been greatly influential in recent political thought. However, it has also overshadowed the independently original contributions of his two primary theoretical sources, Carl Schmitt and Walter Benjamin. In this article, I argue that Agamben’s political defeatism can be traced back to a double misconception in his reception of these two authors: first a formalistic reduction of Schmitt, and second a Schmittian reduction of Benjamin. Through this reduction to juridical formalism, the radicality of Benjamin’s historical materialist critique is replaced by a messianic nihilism, leading to the colonization of society by the logic of the concentration camp. Though Agamben’s theory of sovereignty and its implications for a positive political project have been contested, this origin of the decline of radical philosophy has not previously undergone a systematic critique.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{khatib2013messianic,
  author = {Khatib, Sami},
  title = {The Messianic Without Messianism: Walter Benjamin's Materialist Theology},
  journal = {Anthropology \& Materialism},
  year = {2013},
  volume = {1},
  doi = {10.4000/am.159},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Khatib argues that Benjamin's messianism is irreducibly bound to his materialism in a way that transforms both: it is neither orthodox Marxist materialism (which it critiques as progressivist) nor religious messianism (which it secularizes). The resulting "materialist theology" is characterized by what Khatib calls "the messianic without messianism" -- a structural, formal messianic index that orients historical-materialist critique without positing a positive eschatological content.

  RELEVANCE: This article is central to understanding how the puppet-dwarf image functions: theology is not an external supplement to materialism but a structural condition that transforms the meaning of historical materialist practice. Khatib's analysis directly bears on whether the dwarf "runs" the puppet or is merely its hidden fuel.

  POSITION: The materialism/theology binary is a false dichotomy in Benjamin; both terms are transformed in their encounter such that the result cannot be classified under either rubric alone.
  },
  keywords = {messianism, historical-materialism, theology, Jetztzeit, Khatib, High},
  abstract = {This article discusses Walter Benjamin’s messianic thought. Far from being a theological concept proper or a secularized motif of Judeo-Christian religion, the messianic is a complex figure of thought addressing a dimension of profane life that is neither culture nor nature but a ‘weak power’ within history allowing for a messianic standstill of the self-totalizing and self-eternalizing progress of capitalist ‘real-history’. If the messianic is not about religion or political theology but concerns the profane order of the profane, how are we to conceive of it in a non-reductionist way? The underlying question of this article hence is: if the messianic is the theological name for something within the realm of the profane that is neither addressed by political philosophy nor life and social sciences, what is its relevance for a materialist understanding of history, historical time, and revolutionary politics?},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  sep_context = {SEP (Section 5, Politics): "the messianic would rather appear to point to the 'irreducible theological remainder' upon which Benjamin's concept of redemption rests, one that Khatib (2013) argues should not be reduced to that of Judaeo-Christian theology."},
}

@article{marramao2008messianism,
  author = {Marramao, Giacomo},
  title = {Messianism without Delay: On the ``Post-religious'' Political Theology of Walter Benjamin},
  journal = {Constellations},
  year = {2008},
  volume = {15},
  number = {3},
  pages = {397--405},
  doi = {10.1111/j.1467-8675.2008.00500.x},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Marramao characterizes Benjamin's political theology as "post-religious" -- a form of messianism that preserves the structure of theological interruption (the arrival of the messianic moment) while evacuating doctrinal religious content. Benjamin's "messianism without delay" refers to the urgency of political action in the Jetztzeit that is not deferred to a future Kingdom but erupts within secular historical time.

  RELEVANCE: This article directly addresses whether Benjamin's theological commitments are genuinely religious or function as a secular-political vocabulary. Marramao's "post-religious" reading offers a middle path between the Lowy (functional messianism) and Jacobson (genuine theology) positions, and helps characterize what is distinctive about Benjamin's contribution to political theology debates.

  POSITION: Benjamin is neither a Marxist who uses theology instrumentally nor a theologian with political concerns, but a theorist of a specifically modern secular messianism whose structure is irreducibly theological in form.
  },
  keywords = {messianism, political-theology, secularism, Jetztzeit, post-religious, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{jacobson2003metaphysics,
  author = {Jacobson, Eric L.},
  title = {Metaphysics of the Profane: The Political Theology of Walter Benjamin and Gershom Scholem},
  year = {2003},
  publisher = {Columbia University Press},
  address = {New York},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Jacobson reconstructs the intellectual relationship between Benjamin and Scholem from the early Weimar period, arguing that their shared commitments to Jewish messianism, divine justice, and the critique of law provide the theological grounding for Benjamin's later historical-political thought. The book reads Benjamin's early writings on language, justice, and the critique of violence as constitutively theological rather than instrumentally so.

  RELEVANCE: This is the major book-length treatment of Benjamin as a political theologian in the strong sense -- theology is not merely a vehicle for materialist politics but the substantive basis of Benjamin's critique. It provides the strongest counterpoint to the Lowy/Marxist reading and is essential background for understanding the Theological-Political Fragment's place in Benjamin's development.

  POSITION: Benjamin's theological commitments (to Jewish messianism and the idea of divine justice) are primary and fundamental; his Marxism is a later, somewhat discordant addition that never fully displaced the theological core.
  },
  keywords = {political-theology, Scholem, messianism, justice, language, Jewish-mysticism, High},
  abstract = {The powerful qualities view is most commonly associated with C.B. Martin (1997) and John Heil’s (2003) “identity theory”, on which properties are identical to both their powerfulness and their qualitativity. This identity claim has struck many philosophers as deeply puzzling. Witness David Armstrong’s (2005: 315) reaction: “I confess that I find this totally incredible. If anything is a category mistake, it is a category mistake to identify a quality—a categorical property—and a power, essentially something that points to a certain effect.” Contemporary defenders of the powerful qualities view often share at least some of this scepticism toward Martin and Heil’s theory, while also thinking that the core idea that properties are, in some significant sense, both powerful and qualitative is worth defending. Consequently, there have recently been various proposals for updated, and hopefully less puzzling, versions of the view.
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

Vassilis Livanios’sThe Metaphysics of Powerful Qualitiesis a welcome addition to this literature. Part I of the book provides a critical survey of extant powerful qualities views, beginning with the identity view and moving on to more recent proposals. While Livanios’s discussion of these views is generally charitable, he ultimately argues that each of them runs into significant difficulties. In Part II of the book, he develops and defends a novel powerful qualities view that he terms “powerful categoricalism”. Part I is not simply background for Part II, because an important part of Livanios’s argument for powerful categoricalism is that it avoids the problems he raises for other powerful qualities views in Part I. So, his arguments in Part I constitute an important part of his overall argument for the positive proposal in Part II. In Part II, he develops a second argument for his view based on the idea that it constitutes part of a compelling account of the laws of nature. I will begin by looking at the negative arguments he develops in Part I before turning to his positive proposal and the argument from laws that he gives in Part II.

Part I consists of Chapters 2 to 6 (Chapter 1 is a general introductory chapter that outlines the book’s aims and agenda). In Chapter 2, Livanios introduces powerful qualities in general before introducing and arguing against the identity theory (Chapter 3), grounding views (Chapter 4), compound views (Chapter 5) and higher-order views (Chapter 6) of powerful qualities. Livanios’s discussion of each view is detailed, often mapping out various possible versions of the view, and the arguments he mobilises against the views are wide-ranging. In addition to the role it plays in his argument, this part of the book provides an excellent critical overview of recent work on powerful qualities. Those new to the area will find a very useful introduction here, while those who are familiar with the field will find much worth thinking about. However, while I think Livanios’s careful and charitable discussions are valuable, I am dubious that he establishes that all existing views run into serious problems. In particular, I think grounding views can get around the main problem he raises for them.

The key idea behind grounding views is that properties have qualitative identities or essences, but, nonetheless, ground dispositions. Livanios’s main argument against these views is that they fail to capture a powerful qualities view and, instead, collapse into some other account of properties (Section 4.3.4). His argument works by trying to show that this result follows, given either an operational or a predicational account of grounding. I will focus on his discussion of the predicational account. Given this account, the grounder and the groundee both exist and are independent entities. So, combining the grounding view with this conception of grounding entails that both qualities and dispositions exist and are separate entities. Livanios thinks this result means that the grounding view ultimately entails the existence of distinct qualities and dispositional properties. Consequently, he thinks the grounding view is actually a version of property dualism rather than a powerful qualities view.

It seems to me that proponents of grounding views can respond to this objection by clarifying how they understand powers. Powers have been understood both as properties that are identical to dispositions or clusters of dispositions, and as properties that in some appropriate sense “bestow” dispositions. The grounding view fits naturally with the latter view, as it allows us to say that a property bestows a disposition—and so is a power—just if it grounds dispositions in the right way. The grounding theorist can, then, say that properties are identical with powers, because they ground dispositions in the right way. Because properties have qualitative essences, they are also identical with qualities. The result is a ground-theoretic version of the identity theory, on which properties are identical to both powers and qualities. This seems to me to constitute a robust version of the powerful qualities view. I also do not think this view leads to property dualism properly understood. Within the literature, property dualism is not simply the view that both qualities and dispositions or powers exist but rather the view that they are metaphysically on a par. So, for instance, while orthodox categoricalists allow that dispositions exist, they are still categoricalists rather than property dualists, because they hold that dispositions are less fundamental than categorical properties. Given that grounding theorists hold that dispositions are less fundamental than powerful qualities, they are no more property dualists than orthodox categoricalists are. I do not think, then, that Livanios’s main criticism of grounding views succeeds. If I am right about this, though, Livanios’s first main argument for powerful categoricalism is undermined, because at least one extant version of the powerful qualities view escapes the difficulties Livanios raises for it.

Let me turn now to the argument in the second part of the book, which runs through Chapters 7 to 10. Drawing on Ioannidis, Livanios and Psillos (2021), Livanios begins in Chapter 7 by introducing a “Dual Model” of the laws of nature and arguing that it avoids shortcomings of both power-theoretic and governing-law accounts of laws. The problem for powers views is that they struggle to account for the full range of scientific laws, including functional laws and conservation laws. The problem for governing laws, which Ioannidis, Livanios and Psillos dub the “governing problem”, is that it is obscure how governing laws can “tell” the properties that they involve how to behave.

The Dual Model aims to avoid these problems by positing both nomic relations and powers of a special sort. It avoids the problem for standard powers views by deriving the structure of laws from external nomic relations rather than building it into the natures of first-order powers. The “governing problem” is addressed by ascribing to properties the “ultra-thin” power to enter into nomic relations, and by holding that nomic relations are relata-specific with “slots” for specific qualities. These two points are supposed to give us an account both of why properties are apt to enter nomic relations in general, because they have a power to do so, and why they enter the specific nomic relations that they do, because those relations are relata-specific. So, the Dual Model is supposed to provide an attractive non-Humean account of laws by avoiding the key problems faced by both standard power-theoretic and governing-law views.

Chapter 8 clarifies the conception of properties involved in the Dual Model and explains why it constitutes a version of the powerful qualities view. The core idea is that properties are powerful because they have the ultra-thin power to enter nomic relations, and they are qualities because they depend on external relations to play their specific nomic roles. This is the core of Livanios’s “powerful categoricalist” view of properties. Because this view is implicit in the Dual Model, the attractiveness of the dual model provides motivation for powerful categoricalism.},
}

@article{goldstein2001messianism,
  author = {Goldstein, Warren S.},
  title = {Messianism and Marxism: Walter Benjamin and Ernst Bloch's Dialectical Theories of Secularization},
  journal = {Critical Sociology},
  year = {2001},
  volume = {27},
  number = {2},
  pages = {246--281},
  doi = {10.1177/08969205010270020501},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Goldstein analyzes the parallel deployment of messianism in Benjamin and Bloch as a dialectical theory of secularization: both thinkers refuse the simple secularization thesis (religion disappears in modernity) by showing how theological content is preserved, transformed, and reactivated within materialist political thought. For Benjamin, the "weak messianic power" transmitted across generations exemplifies this dialectical preservation of theological structure within secular historical consciousness.

  RELEVANCE: This comparative study illuminates the broader context of Benjamin's theological-materialist synthesis by situating it alongside Bloch's utopian messianism, clarifying what is distinctive about each and how both diverge from orthodox Marxism's atheistic materialism. It bears directly on understanding why Benjamin turned to theology rather than simply advancing his Marxist critique.

  POSITION: Both Benjamin and Bloch represent a coherent tradition of dialectical secularization that synthesizes Marxist and religious thought; their messianism is neither purely religious nor purely secular but a third, genuinely dialectical phenomenon.
  },
  keywords = {messianism, Marxism, Bloch, secularization, weak-messianic-power, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{tagliacozzo2022messianism,
  author = {Tagliacozzo, Tamara},
  title = {Messianism and Happiness in Walter Benjamin's Theological-Political Fragment},
  journal = {Aisthesis. Pratiche, linguaggi e saperi dell'estetico},
  year = {2022},
  volume = {15},
  number = {1},
  pages = {91--100},
  doi = {10.36253/aisthesis-13117},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Tagliacozzo argues that the Theological-Political Fragment's concept of happiness illuminates a structural parallel between Benjamin and Kant: both articulate a "redemptive pessimism" in which the profane pursuit of happiness and the messianic order run in opposite directions yet together constitute a rhythm of history. The fragment's argument that "nature is messianic by reason of its eternal and total caducity" positions happiness as the transient, secular mode through which the messianic indirectly operates.

  RELEVANCE: The Theological-Political Fragment is the key text for understanding how Benjamin relates the profane and messianic orders -- a question that directly structures the puppet-dwarf image. This article's Kantian reading offers a bridge between Benjamin's early theological writings and his mature Marxist-messianic synthesis in the Theses.

  POSITION: The Fragment positions theology and the secular order in a relation of inverse parallelism rather than simple instrumentality: each enables the other while moving in opposite directions.
  },
  keywords = {theological-political-fragment, happiness, messianism, Kant, profane, Medium},
  abstract = {On the basis of a citation from the final part of Walter Benjamin’s Theological-Political Fragment (1920-21), we shall hypothesize a similarity between Benjamin and Kant, the political thinker and philosopher of history, in his Idea of a Universal History with a Cosmopolitan Intent. According to Kant, the man who participates in history and in the human race seeks happiness and the means to procure it for himself as a member of «free» humanity, thus seeking to unify external freedom and happiness in institutions of law. During the period 1920-1921 Benjamin was strongly critical of the concept of law, including also that of Kant, which he saw as contradictory to the idea of justice; in opposition, Benjamin held first an anarchist-libertarian view, then a revolutionary one. From a dialectical perspective, however, we find in common in Benjamin and Kant a redemptive pessimism that foresees, in the «twisted wood» of mankind, an ultimate providential and messianic possibility: «The spiritual restitutio in integrum, that leads to immortality, correspond to a worldly restitution that leads to an eternity of downfall, and the rhythm of this eternally transient worldly existence, transient in its totality, in its spatial but also in its temporal totality», the eternal succession of generations and their institutions, which is «the rhythm of messianic nature», is happiness. Nature eternalizes itself in this virtual spatial and temporal totality in the history of humanity moving toward the realization of the idea of law and, spatially, the achievement of global, cosmopolitan politics: «nature is messianic by reason of its eternal and total caducity».},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{hay2024unlearning,
  author = {Hay, Natasha},
  title = {Unlearning Pauline Messianism: Prophetic Study and Historical Injustice in Walter Benjamin and Hermann Cohen},
  journal = {The Journal of Speculative Philosophy},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {38},
  number = {3},
  pages = {313--323},
  doi = {10.5325/jspecphil.38.3.0313},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Hay challenges Agamben's Pauline reading of Benjamin's theopolitics by recovering Benjamin's critical inheritance of Hermann Cohen's Jewish ethics -- specifically the concepts of unintentional sin and atonement. She argues that this Cohenian-prophetic register (Talmud Torah, attention to the cultural afterlives of historical violence) is incompatible with the antinomian, anarcholibertarian messianism Agamben projects onto Benjamin.

  RELEVANCE: This recent article intervenes in the dominant reception of Benjamin's messianism by showing that its Jewish sources are Cohenian-normative rather than Pauline-antinomian, which directly affects how one reads the "weak messianic power" and the alliance of theology with historical materialism. It also situates Benjamin within debates in political theology that go beyond the Schmitt-Benjamin opposition.

  POSITION: Benjamin's theopolitics belongs to the prophetic-normative tradition of Jewish ethics, not to the antinomian-messianic tradition; this affects the meaning of both his theology and his Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {messianism, Cohen, Agamben, Paul, Jewish-ethics, political-theology, Medium},
  abstract = {abstract:This article argues against Giorgio Agamben’s use of Paul’s Epistles to align Walter Benjamin’s critique of violence with antinomian theology and anarcholibertarian politics. It focuses specifically on Benjamin’s critical inheritance of Hermann Cohen’s concepts of unintentional sin and atonement to show that he reactivates a prophetic register of education in Jewish religious ethics. The article contends that these normative and narrative elements of Talmud Torah refute a reductive view of Jewish legalism in confronting the cultural afterlives of historical violence. This argument not only uncouples the reception of Benjamin’s theopolitics from contemporary continental philosophy’s revivals of Pauline messianism, but also contributes to a plural field of political theology emerging beyond the scope of secularized concepts of governance derived from a spurious universalism. This article proposes that the nonidentity between human law and divine justice can orient necessary resistance to justifications for and acts of state violence committed in a community’s name.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@incollection{lowy2019jewish,
  author = {L{\"o}wy, Michael},
  title = {Walter Benjamin and Jewish Radical Culture},
  booktitle = {Oxford Research Encyclopedia of Literature},
  year = {2019},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  doi = {10.1093/acrefore/9780190201098.013.44},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: L{\"o}wy situates Benjamin within a constellation of early-twentieth-century Central European Jewish radical intellectuals (Lukacs, Bloch, Landauer) characterized by an "elective affinity" between German Romanticism, Jewish messianism, and revolutionary politics. Benjamin is described as a "religious atheist with anarchist leanings" who only discovers Marxism in the mid-1920s through Lukacs's History and Class Consciousness, and who then becomes the first Marxist to break radically with the ideology of progress.

  RELEVANCE: This entry provides essential intellectual-historical context for the puppet-dwarf image: it shows that Benjamin's theological commitments predate and help shape his Marxism, rather than being grafted onto it. The "elective affinities" framework explains why the combination of messianism and historical materialism is not contradictory but reflects a specific milieu's synthesis of emancipatory traditions.

  POSITION: Benjamin's Marxism is a late addition to an already-formed messianic-romantic worldview; the encounter produces heterodox Marxism that is philosophically superior to orthodox materialism precisely because of its theological supplement.
  },
  keywords = {Jewish-messianism, romanticism, Marxism, heterodox, Lukacs, Bloch, Medium},
  abstract = {Walter Benjamin (1892–1940) was situated among a constellation of early-20th-century radical Jewish thinkers delving into questions of German culture and philosophy in Mitteleuropa. Within this Jewish Central European radical culture, a complex network of links, of “elective affinities,” as Johann Wolfgang Goethe called them, brought together romanticism, Jewish messianism, anti-bourgeois cultural rebellion, and revolutionary (socialist and anarchist) utopias. This messianism is not the one of Jewish orthodoxy but a new, highly political version, seen through the lens of German romanticism. Benjamin should thus be viewed as a religious atheist with anarchist leanings, who only discovers Marxism in the mid-1920s, following the lectures of Georg Lukacs’s that were published as History and Class Consciousness in 1923. He became the first Marxist to break radically with the ideology of progress. Benjamin’s thinking has a distinct critical quality that sets his apart from the dominant and official forms of historical materialism and gives him a formidable political and intellectual superiority as a Marxist critic. This philosophical peculiarity comes from his ability to incorporate into the body of Marxist revolutionary theory insights from Jewish messianism and from the German Romantic critique of modern civilization.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{khanlarzadeh2020theology,
  author = {Khanlarzadeh, Mina},
  title = {Theology of Revolution: In Ali Shari'ati and Walter Benjamin's Political Thought},
  journal = {Religions},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {11},
  number = {10},
  pages = {504},
  doi = {10.3390/rel11100504},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Khanlarzadeh offers a comparative analysis of Benjamin and Iranian revolutionary thinker Ali Shari'ati, arguing that both created theological-political conceptions of revolution in which theology and Marxism are not merely combined but dialectically transformed by each other: theology is animated by historical-materialist analysis, and Marxism is charged with the redemptive force of religious remembrance. Both thinkers theorize an "active messianism" in which every present moment carries messianic potential.

  RELEVANCE: The comparative structure illuminates what is structurally specific to Benjamin's synthesis of theology and Marxism (particularly his concepts of remembrance/Eingedenken and the present as moment of revolutionary possibility) by showing how similar syntheses arose independently in non-Western revolutionary contexts. This broadens the domain beyond the Eurocentric reception.

  POSITION: Benjamin's theological-political synthesis is not merely a European-Jewish phenomenon but exemplifies a broader form of revolutionary thought that combines materialist analysis with theological motivation.
  },
  keywords = {theology, revolution, messianism, Marxism, Shari-ati, comparative, Medium},
  abstract = {In this paper, I offer a comparative analysis of the political thoughts of twentieth century Iranian revolutionary thinker and sociologist Ali Shari’ati (1933–1977) and German-Jewish philosopher Walter Benjamin (1892–1940). Despite their conspicuously independent historical-theoretical trajectories, both Shari’ati and Benjamin engaged with theology and Marxism to create theological–political conceptions of the revolution of the oppressed. Shari’ati re-interpreted and re-animated Shia history from the angle of contemporary concerns to theorize a revolution against all forms of domination. In comparison, Benjamin fused Marxism with Jewish theology in his call to seize the possibilities of past failed revolutions in the present. Both Shari’ati and Benjamin conceptualized an active messianism led by each generation, eliminating the wait for the return of a messiah. As a result, each present moment takes on a messianic potential; the present plays an essential role to both thinkers. Past was also essential to both, because theology (through remembrance) had made the past sufferings incomplete to them. Both thinkers viewed past sufferings as an integral part of present struggles for justice in the form of remembrance (or yād or zekr for Shari’ati, and Zekher for Benjamin). I explore the ways Shari’ati and Benjamin theorized the role of the past in the present, remembrance, and messianism to create a dialectical relation between theology and Marxism to reciprocally transform and compliment both of them.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{simpson2021migrants,
  author = {Simpson, Sid},
  title = {Migrants in the Profane: Critical Theory and the Question of Secularization},
  journal = {Political Theology},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {22},
  pages = {350--352},
  doi = {10.1080/1462317x.2021.1917223},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This review of Peter Gordon's book Migrants in the Profane synthesizes Gordon's argument that Benjamin represents a theorist of "ambivalent secularization" -- the hidden puppet operator (theology) is not eliminated but concealed and transformed in historical materialism. Gordon's worry that extra-historical theology risks devolving into Schmittian decisionism is noted as the key critical stakes of Benjamin's move.

  RELEVANCE: The review reconstructs the puppet-dwarf image's logic with precision: theology does not run independently of historical materialism but constitutes its animating concealed operator, rendering Benjamin's secularization processual and dialectical rather than terminal. Gordon's charge of potential Schmittian decisionism raises the question of whether theological "weak force" can be critically controlled within materialism.

  POSITION: Benjamin occupies an unstable position "between Marxism and messianism" that is productive as critique but potentially dangerous if theology escapes the discipline of materialist analysis.
  },
  keywords = {secularization, Gordon, puppet-dwarf, Schmitt, Critical-Theory, Low},
  abstract = {The Frankfurt School’s considerable indebtedness to Marx’s historical materialism and Weber’s reflections on the “disenchantment of the world” make for an unexpected critical resource for thinking through the question of secularization. InMigrants in the Profane: Critical Theory and the Question of Secularization, Peter E. Gordon finds inWalter Benjamin, Max Horkheimer, and Theodor Adorno distinct attempts to theorize how modern secularization required religion’s transformation rather than its mere disappearance. As Adorno explained in a 1963 letter to Gershom Scholem; “Nothing of theological content will persist without being transformed; every content will have to put itself to the test of migrating into the realm of the secular, the profane” (111–112). The book, adapted from Gordon’s 2017 Franz Rosenzweig Lectures in Jewish Thought at Yale University, interrogates each of the three Frankfurt School critical theorists’ approach to theology. First is Benjamin, whose well known thesis on history and Paul Klee’s Angelus Novus already betrays the uneasy admixture of theology and historical materialism at the center of his thought. Gordon looks to Benjamin’s image of the undefeatable chess-playing mechanical Turk as a cipher for his position on theology. Though it appeared to function by virtue of its ingenious technical creation, the automaton in truth concealed a human operator. In Benjamin’s reading, the mechanical puppet is “historical materialism,” while the hidden flesh and blood operator is “theology, which today, as we know, is wizened and has to keep out of sight.” In other words, Benjamin contends that an extra-historical concept of the messianic animates historical materialism, and paradoxically renders secularization a process of theological concealment rather than disappearance. This is no genuinemessianism but rather what Benjamin calls a “weak messianic power”: a dialectical transformation of theology that animates motivation to revolution. On this basis, Gordon declares Benjamin a theorist of “ambivalent secularization, poised in indecision between Marxism and messianism” (53). Gordon’s worry, however, is that an appeal to an extra-historical principle that escapes critical scrutiny risks devolving into Schmittian decisionism. In Gordon’s mind, Horkheimer’s position on the role of theology transformed greatly throughout his life. The young Horkheimer dismissed any metaphysical considerations outside of material history, theological or otherwise. The older Horkheimer who co-authored with Adorno Dialectic of Enlightenment held a more ambivalent view: that Judaism “signified both an emancipatory principle opposed to the world-immanence of myth and a model for world-transcendent reason whose appearance marked an inaugural state in the dialectic of enlightenment” (145). On his view, monotheism became Janus-faced in modernity: it represented an alternative to the instrumental rationality that structured the emergence of Western subjectivity, despite its formative role in that history. Later still, this ambivalence would ultimately give way to a straightforward appeal to theology as “a final refuge for critical resistance to the supremacy of the merely given” (93). That theism alone sustained “the longing for something other than this world” (89) was trouble for Horkheimer’s commitment},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{ostovich1994messianic,
  author = {Ostovich, Steven T.},
  title = {Messianic History in Benjamin and Metz},
  journal = {Philosophy and Theology},
  year = {1994},
  volume = {8},
  number = {4},
  pages = {271--289},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ostovich draws a parallel between Benjamin's messianic philosophy of history and Johann Baptist Metz's political theology, arguing that both conceive history as the site of eschatological interruption by "dangerous memories" of human suffering. However, Ostovich notes that Metz's explicitly theological categories help avoid what he sees as the dualism internal to Benjamin's simultaneous description of history in both messianic and materialist terms.

  RELEVANCE: This article represents an early attempt to situate Benjamin's theological commitments within a systematic theological framework (political theology after Metz), which illuminates the theological ambitions of the Theses independently of the Marxist reception. It also pinpoints the dualism problem that later interpreters (Khatib, Marramao) attempt to resolve.

  POSITION: Benjamin's messianic history is genuinely theological in structure, comparable to Christian political theology; the Marxist frame is secondary and generates internal tensions that theological analysis can clarify.
  },
  keywords = {messianism, Metz, political-theology, dangerous-memories, dualism, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@incollection{khatib2024benjamins,
  author = {Khatib, Sami},
  title = {Benjamin's Materialism: Anthropological, Historical, or Dialectical?},
  booktitle = {The Palgrave Walter Benjamin Handbook},
  editor = {Ross, Nathan},
  year = {2024},
  publisher = {Springer Nature Switzerland},
  address = {Cham},
  pages = {579--593},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Khatib maps the three major strands of Benjamin's materialism -- anthropological (image space and body space), historical (ideology as phantasmagoria and dialectical image), and dialectical (commodity fetishism and Marx's value theory) -- and argues that Benjamin's heterodox materialism is irreducible to any one of them. The chapter traces the Adorno-Benjamin debates of the 1930s over the relation between "theological" and "materialist" modes and shows how Benjamin navigated between Frankfurt School orthodoxy and Brechtian vulgar Marxism.

  RELEVANCE: This handbook chapter provides systematic coverage of Benjamin's materialism across his career, directly contextualizing the Theses' puppet-dwarf image within the broader trajectory of Benjamin's materialist method. It situates the theology-materialism relation within the Adorno-Benjamin correspondence, a crucial context for understanding the Theses' composition.

  POSITION: Benjamin's materialism is "neither Frankfurt nor Brechtian nor vulgar" -- it constitutes a genuinely new materialist position that can only be understood through its entanglement with theological motifs.
  },
  keywords = {materialism, Adorno, dialectical-image, phantasmagoria, commodity-fetishism, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{loveluck2011redemption,
  author = {Loveluck, Benjamin},
  title = {The Redemption of Experience: On Walter Benjamin's `Hermeneutical Materialism'},
  journal = {Philosophy and Social Criticism},
  year = {2011},
  volume = {37},
  number = {2},
  pages = {167--188},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Loveluck argues that Benjamin developed a distinctive "hermeneutical materialism" that combined theological interpretation with dialectical materialism, always grounding hermeneutics in concrete social-historical life. In refusing both Heideggerian authenticity and orthodox Gadamerian hermeneutics, Benjamin fashioned a materially grounded redemption of human experience through the "mimetic faculty" of re-interpretation -- what Loveluck reads as the practical-political meaning of theology's role in historical materialism.

  RELEVANCE: This article illuminates how the theological dimension of Benjamin's materialism operates at the level of hermeneutic practice (reading the past, redeeming experience, unlocking frozen possibilities) rather than merely at the level of theoretical postulation. This connects the puppet-dwarf image to Benjamin's practice as a critic and historian.

  POSITION: Theology in Benjamin is a hermeneutic operator, a mode of reading that materializes eschatological energy in the form of redemptive re-interpretation of experience.
  },
  keywords = {hermeneutics, materialism, experience, mimesis, Gadamer, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}
